rnment camp all the city's garbage  from traditional trash  whale bones and corpses go in here  there's also quite a lot of infected  people here too  as anyone who agrees to the royal  spymaster's rehabilitation program  whether they've volunteered or not  is send here to be quarantined away from  everyone the disease is going to kill  them anyway so why waste the whale oil  to drive their corpses over to the  district when you could just put them in  the district to begin with  it's like digging your own grave except  this time it's already dug the moment  you're dragged in here of course the  district is surrounded by large arc  pylons walls of light and other  machinery to prevent people from  escaping  they are forcing the people to stay in  the district and give them no option to  leave  like i said you're dead the moment you  walk in and that rail car comes quite  frequently you could say this is done on  purpose as this is a way to escape the  district so it's more of a gameplay  mechanic but if we were to shut that out  of our minds for a moment  it's insane how fast this comes back  with the number of bodies it has  every couple of minutes or so it comes  by and pours dozens of corpses into the  district letting them rot and it keeps  coming back with more and more bodies  i did do a bit of research for this on  the wikipedias i couldn't find any info  in game but apparently dunwall had a  population of around 2 million before  the plague and  currently in-game is around 200 000  people left so just think about that for  a moment there are tons of areas like  this  in fact all areas are very much the same  with having run down buildings  patrolling guards and dead bodies along  the streets  but i want to dial it back to the chaos  discussion as i said earlier the chaos  level determines the ending of the game  and while it also changes the ending  mission  the after credit narration is what i  want to focus on more should the player  kill little to no one 